This repository holds a couple of Python scripts built to streamline neuroimaging workflows. They handle everything from raw anatomical surface reconstruction using Charm to running personalized SimNIBS tDCS simulations and extracting ROI stats.

1. run_charm_T1-T2.py

Batch script that automates head mesh creation using Charm.   
  What it does: 
    Loops through subject directories (sub-XXXX/ses-base/anat/) looking for T1w and T2w structural scans.   
    Skips completed work: Automatically checks if an m2m folder already exists for a subject so it doesn't re-run charm unnecessarily.   
    Clean execution: Ignores hidden/corrupted macOS files (like ._*) and fires off charm subjectXXXX <T1> <T2> --forceqform directly in the subject folder.  


2. Sim_pipeline_V6.py

The main simulation runner (For questions: Christian Curiel, curielchris05@gmail.com). 
It sets up and executes 4-channel tDCS simulations via SimNIBS (acorrding with the aim of MeMoSLAP P2), extracts field magnitudes, and spits out a unified CSV (sim_results_V6.csv).

The script does the following:
    Checks disk space for existing completed volume meshes and fsaverage overlays before running. 
    If a simulation is already done, it skips the heavy math and goes straight to data extraction.   
  Dynamic Electrode Mapping: 
      Pulls subject-space (X, Y, Z) coordinates directly from a target CSV file for anode and cathode setups (obtained prior).   
      Multi-Space ROI Extraction:
        Volume (MNI): Samples electric fields (E) and current density (J) across volumetric NIfTI masks in MNI space.   
        Surface (fsaverage): Maps cortical patches across hemispheres using FreeSurfer overlays.   
  Harmonization Metrics: Calculates volume/area-weighted field averages, total surface areas, and integrated electric fields. 
  It also measures tissue exposure above key intensity thresholds (like 0.10, 0.15, and 0.20 V/m).
